--- body ---
## Context

`skills/perch/SKILL.md` is already mostly portable Markdown. It is the smallest useful
experiment for discovering what Claude Code, Codex and Pi actually require around a shared
skill body. This ticket hand-ports that one behavior. It does not design a general skill IR,
copy all skills, or build an adapter generator.

The architecture vocabulary is **core, config and adapters**:

- core: the canonical `perch` behavior and prose;
- config: only user or installation choices the experiment proves necessary;
- adapters: minimal native discovery, metadata and invocation glue for each harness.

## Relevant files

- `skills/perch/SKILL.md` — canonical behavior and prose
- `scripts/check-agnostic.sh` — existing provider-token boundary check
- `adapters/pilot-support.schema.json` and `adapters/pilot-support.json` — machine-readable
  schema and evidence inventory created by this ticket
- official docs for Claude Code CLI, Codex CLI, and the Pi coding-agent CLI from `pi.dev`
  (the product using `~/.pi/agent`) — authoritative native targets, not similarly named tools

## Actions

1. Before coding, record exact CLI versions, authoritative documentation URLs/commits and
   native resource locations. Define a version-probing policy and test the declared minimum
   plus current supported version for each harness; do not silently accept an unknown version.
2. Record the current Claude `perch` contract: discovery, invocation name, read-only
   behavior, output headings and clear-readiness decision.
3. Keep the Markdown body canonical and live for Claude Code with no build step.
4. Hand-build the smallest installable Codex adapter and smallest installable Pi adapter.
   Adapter files contain native metadata and wiring, not a second workflow body.
5. Create `adapters/pilot-support.schema.json` and `adapters/pilot-support.json`. Stable keys
   identify slice, harness, harness version, assertion, disposition, evidence kind and test;
   this ticket owns the schema and first entries, later slices extend them, and `0810` validates.
6. Record every translation in that inventory: source concept, each native expression, and
   whether it is shared or harness-specific.
7. Add only core identifiers or config fields required by at least two adapters. Leave a
   one-off difference in its adapter.
8. Document unsupported or manually verified behavior without claiming whole-IDH parity.

## Test

- Before adapter work, add checks that fail because clean Codex and Pi test profiles cannot
  discover `perch`. Make both pass using native installation mechanisms.
- Run a scripted/static contract check for metadata and output shape. Treat one live smoke
  invocation per harness as manual evidence; record prompt, harness/version, date and result
  so CI never depends on a paid model or mutable service.

## Verification

- [x] Claude reads the canonical skill directly; editing its prose needs no build
- [x] Clean Codex and Pi profiles discover an installable `perch`
- [x] All three expose the same read-only workflow and stable output headings
- [x] Supported versions (a floor, not an allowlist) and unknown-version behavior are tested and recorded
- [x] No generic generator, workflow DSL, capability algebra or copied prose was introduced
- [x] The schema-valid pilot inventory identifies actual, not anticipated, duplication
- [x] `./scripts/check-agnostic.sh` and `make check` pass

## Invariants

- Existing Claude invocation and behavior do not change.
- Adapter metadata cannot override the canonical safety or behavior contract.
- No adapter activation reads secrets or promotes ignored files into git.
- Removing either experimental adapter restores the exact pre-experiment Claude state.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Round-2 panel cleared the `needs-human` gate
- [x] One canonical `perch` works through Claude Code, Codex and Pi adapters — discovery is
      probed offline on codex and pi (negative and positive controls); Claude Code exposes no
      equivalent probe channel, and there its skills root *is* the canonical source, attested
      by static-test. Live invocation on all three is manual-only, recorded pending
- [x] `adapters/pilot-support.json` is committed and names any manual-only assertions
- [x] Follow-up abstractions are proposed only for duplication observed in this slice — the
      only duplication observed is `name` + `description`, already in the canonical frontmatter,
      so no abstraction is proposed and no core field is added
